   "source": [
    "## 5.1 Value based segmentation   <a class=\"anchor\" id=\"Flights-EDA\"></a>\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "73127c9d",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "In this section, we examine the data through the lens of **Value-Based Segmentation**.  \n",
    "The analysis focuses on customers’ **income** and **Customer Lifetime Value (CLV)** to identify patterns that may indicate different levels of economic contribution and overall value to the airline.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "e9060e76",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 5.1.1 Income-Based Segmentation <a class=\"anchor\" id=\"Flights-EDA\"></a>\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "a3a17e66",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "We begin with **income-based segmentation** by defining income ranges and assigning customers to these groups. The ranges are anchored to the **median income in Canada (~40,000 CAD)**, where approximately 50% of the population earns below this threshold and 50% above.\n",
    "\n",
    "> **Assumption:** The dataset does not specify currency. For consistency with the benchmark, we assume all income values are expressed in **CAD**.\n",
    "\n",
    "After grouping customers into income bands, we analyze the composition and characteristics of each group to surface patterns relevant for value-oriented decision-making.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 58,
   "id": "eeb1b33a",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Income_Group\n",
      "Very Low     6377\n",
      "Low          2051\n",
      "Medium       1577\n",
      "High         2054\n",
      "Very High    4515\n",
      "Name: count, dtype: int64\n"
     ]
    },
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 1000x600 with 1 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# Create a new column and groupBy Income ranges\n",
    "\n",
    "ranked = customers['Income'].rank(method='first')  # bricht Ties deterministisch\n",
    "customers['Income_Group'] = pd.qcut(\n",
    "    ranked, q=5, labels=['Very Low','Low','Medium','High','Very High']\n",
    ")\n",
    "\n",
    "# Get the median Income from Statistca aroung 40k median income\n",
    "# https://www.statista.com/statistics/464087/median-annual-earnings-in-canada/?srsltid=AfmBOorobKVMkA5XzryvSxoVhMDd2nOgjsvVJ3QwOdzoxHITF-YTxYi1\n",
    "median_income = 40000\n",
    "\n",
    "# Define custom bins around the median\n",
    "bins = [0, 25000, 35000, 45000, 60000, customers['Income'].max()]\n",
    "\n",
    "labels = ['Very Low', 'Low', 'Medium', 'High', 'Very High']\n",
    "\n",
    "# Categorize based on custom bins\n",
    "customers['Income_Group'] = pd.cut(customers['Income'], bins=bins, labels=labels, include_lowest=True)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Check the distribution\n",
    "print(customers['Income_Group'].value_counts().sort_index())\n",
    "\n",
    "# Optional: check mean/median per group\n",
    "#print(customers.groupby('Income_Group')['Income'].mean())\n",
    "#print(customers.groupby('Income_Group')['Income'].median())\n",
    "\n",
    "# Plot the distribution of Income_Group\n",
    "plt.figure(figsize=(10, 6))\n",
    "sns.countplot(data=customers, x='Income_Group', order=labels, palette='Set1', hue='Income_Group', dodge=False)\n",
    "plt.title('Distribution of Income Groups')\n",
    "plt.xlabel('Income Group')\n",
    "plt.ylabel('Number of Customers')\n",
    "plt.show()\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "f1f0ec86",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Observations on Income Group Distribution\n",
    "\n",
    "The income segmentation reveals a **bimodal pattern**: many customers fall into the **very low** and **high** income groups, while the **middle** income range is comparatively underrepresented. Although one might expect a more bell-shaped distribution, this pattern suggests the airline may be attracting customers at both ends of the income spectrum. The underlying drivers remain unclear and warrant **further analysis** (e.g., pricing, route network, product mix, promotions).\n",
    "\n",
    "> **Note on interpretation:** Because the income groups are **unevenly distributed**, we anticipate similar imbalance in related feature distributions. We deliberately **do not normalize** group sizes here to preserve the **true empirical distribution** and avoid masking real patterns; results should be interpreted with this imbalance in mind.\n"
